    <section id="clustering" class="case-section ml-section">
  <p class="eyebrow"><span class="num">05</span> UNSUPERVISED LEARNING</p>

  <div class="ml-intro">
    <div>
      <h2>Can job postings reveal natural career groupings without relying on job titles?</h2>

      <p class="wide-copy">
        Job titles are not always consistent across employers. I used K-Means clustering
        to explore whether data-related job postings would naturally form groups based
        on their underlying characteristics rather than their predefined role labels.
      </p>

      <p class="wide-copy">
        K-Means is an unsupervised learning algorithm, so the model was not given a
        target variable to predict. Instead, it searched for similarity across salary,
        minimum experience, education, and engineered technical-skill indicators.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="ml-method-card">
      <span class="method-label">MODEL</span>
      <strong>K-Means</strong>
      <p>Unsupervised clustering</p>
      <div class="method-tags">
        <span>PySpark ML</span>
        <span>k = 4</span>
        <span>PCA</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>


  <div class="ml-subsection">
    <p class="mini-eyebrow">MODEL PIPELINE</p>
    <h3>From job postings to interpretable clusters</h3>

    <div class="ml-pipeline">
      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <div class="pipeline-icon"><i class="bi bi-funnel"></i></div>
        <span>01</span>
        <strong>Filter</strong>
        <p>Focus on relevant data roles</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pipeline-arrow">→</div>

      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <div class="pipeline-icon"><i class="bi bi-sliders"></i></div>
        <span>02</span>
        <strong>Engineer</strong>
        <p>Create binary skill indicators</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pipeline-arrow">→</div>

      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <div class="pipeline-icon"><i class="bi bi-bar-chart"></i></div>
        <span>03</span>
        <strong>Scale</strong>
        <p>Standardize model features</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pipeline-arrow">→</div>

      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <div class="pipeline-icon"><i class="bi bi-graph-up"></i></div>
        <span>04</span>
        <strong>Select k</strong>
        <p>Compare WSSSE solutions</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pipeline-arrow">→</div>

      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <div class="pipeline-icon"><i class="bi bi-diagram-3"></i></div>
        <span>05</span>
        <strong>Cluster</strong>
        <p>Fit the K-Means model</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pipeline-arrow">→</div>

      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <div class="pipeline-icon"><i class="bi bi-search"></i></div>
        <span>06</span>
        <strong>Interpret</strong>
        <p>PCA + cluster profiles</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>


  <div class="ml-subsection">
    <p class="mini-eyebrow">01 — FEATURE ENGINEERING</p>
    <h3>Turning skill text into model-ready variables</h3>

    <p class="wide-copy">
      I first combined the available skill fields into a single text representation.
      I then created binary indicators for a selected set of technical skills so that
      each posting could be represented numerically.
    </p>

    <div class="feature-chips">
      <span><i class="bi bi-currency-dollar"></i> Salary</span>
      <span><i class="bi bi-briefcase"></i> Experience</span>
      <span><i class="bi bi-mortarboard"></i> Education</span>
      <span><i class="bi bi-code-slash"></i> Technical Skills</span>
    </div>

    <div class="ml-code-group">
      <div class="code-card">
        <div class="code-toolbar">
          <div class="window-dots"><b></b><b></b><b></b></div>
          <span class="language-pill">PySpark</span>
        </div>
        <pre><code class="language-python"># combine all skill text
from pyspark.sql.functions import concat_ws, coalesce, lit

skill_cols = [
    "SKILLS_NAME",
    "SPECIALIZED_SKILLS_NAME",
    "SOFTWARE_SKILLS_NAME",
    "COMMON_SKILLS_NAME"
]

df_roles = df_roles.withColumn(
    "ALL_SKILLS_TEXT",
    lower(
        concat_ws(
            " ",
            *[coalesce(col(c), lit("")) for c in skill_cols]
        )
    )
)</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="code-card">
        <div class="code-toolbar">
          <div class="window-dots"><b></b><b></b><b></b></div>
          <span class="language-pill">PySpark</span>
        </div>
        <pre><code class="language-python"># create binary skill features
from pyspark.sql.functions import regexp_extract

technical_skills = [
    "python","sql","excel","tableau","power bi",
    "machine learning","artificial intelligence",
    "data analysis","statistics","math",
    "linear algebra","probability","data visualization",
    "spark","aws","computer science"
]

df_skill_features = df_roles

for skill in technical_skills:
    clean_name = skill.replace(" ", "_").replace("-", "_").replace("/", "_")
    pattern = rf" {skill.lower()} "

    df_skill_features = df_skill_features.withColumn(
        f"skill_{clean_name}",
        (regexp_extract(col("ALL_SKILLS_TEXT"), pattern, 0) != "").cast("int")
    )</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>


  <div class="ml-subsection">
    <p class="mini-eyebrow">02 — MODEL FEATURES &amp; PREPROCESSING</p>
    <h3>Putting every feature on a comparable scale</h3>

    <p class="wide-copy">
      The final model combined salary, minimum years of experience, education level,
      and the engineered skill indicators. Missing observations were removed for the
      selected model features before assembling them into a feature vector.
    </p>

    <div class="ml-code-group">
      <div class="code-card">
        <div class="code-toolbar">
          <div class="window-dots"><b></b><b></b><b></b></div>
          <span class="language-pill">PySpark ML</span>
        </div>
        <pre><code class="language-python"># define feature columns
features_cols = [
    "SALARY",
    "MIN_YEARS_EXPERIENCE_CLEANED",
    "MIN_EDULEVELS"
] + [c for c in df_skill_features.columns if c.startswith("skill_")]
# drop missing values
df_model = df_skill_features.dropna(subset=features_cols)
# assmble features
from pyspark.ml.feature import VectorAssembler

assembler = VectorAssembler(
    inputCols=features_cols,
    outputCol="features"
)

df_model = assembler.transform(df_model)</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="ml-callout">
      <div class="callout-icon"><i class="bi bi-rulers"></i></div>
      <div>
        <strong>Why standardize the features?</strong>
        <p>
          K-Means relies on distance. Salary is measured on a much larger numerical
          scale than a binary skill variable, so standardization helps prevent a
          high-magnitude feature from dominating the distance calculation simply
          because of its units.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="ml-code-group">
      <div class="code-card">
        <div class="code-toolbar">
          <div class="window-dots"><b></b><b></b><b></b></div>
          <span class="language-pill">PySpark ML</span>
        </div>
        <pre><code class="language-python">#scale features
from pyspark.ml.feature import StandardScaler

scaler = StandardScaler(
    inputCol="features",
    outputCol="scaled_features",
    withMean=True,
    withStd=True
)

scaler_model = scaler.fit(df_model)
df_model = scaler_model.transform(df_model)</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>


  <div class="ml-subsection">
    <p class="mini-eyebrow">03 — CHOOSING THE NUMBER OF CLUSTERS</p>
    <h3>How many groups best represent the job market?</h3>

    <p class="wide-copy">
      I evaluated candidate values of k using within-cluster sum of squared errors
      (WSSSE). The error declined consistently from k = 2 through k = 4, but there
      was no sharp elbow. That gradual pattern suggested that the postings did not
      naturally separate into perfectly distinct groups.
    </p>

    <div class="ml-code-group">
      <div class="code-card">
        <div class="code-toolbar">
          <div class="window-dots"><b></b><b></b><b></b></div>
          <span class="language-pill">PySpark ML</span>
        </div>
        <pre><code class="language-python"># elbow method
from pyspark.ml.clustering import KMeans

cost = []
k_values = range(2, 5)

for k in k_values:
    kmeans = KMeans(k=k, featuresCol="scaled_features", seed=42)
    model = kmeans.fit(df_model)
    cost.append(model.summary.trainingCost)</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="code-card">
        <div class="code-toolbar">
          <div class="window-dots"><b></b><b></b><b></b></div>
          <span class="language-pill">Matplotlib</span>
        </div>
        <pre><code class="language-python">import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

plt.plot(k_values, cost, marker='o')
plt.xlabel("Number of Clusters (k)")
plt.ylabel("Within Set Sum of Squared Errors (WSSSE)")
plt.title("Elbow Method for Optimal k")
plt.show()</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="ml-decision">
      <div class="decision-number">4</div>
      <div>
        <span>FINAL CLUSTER COUNT</span>
        <h3>Why k = 4?</h3>
        <p>
          I selected four clusters as a balance between model complexity and
          interpretability. Because the WSSSE curve did not contain a dramatic elbow,
          I treat k = 4 as an exploratory modeling choice rather than a definitive
          “true” number of occupational groups.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>


  <div class="ml-subsection">
    <p class="mini-eyebrow">04 — FINAL MODEL</p>

    <div class="analysis-heading">
      <div>
        <h3>Fitting K-Means</h3>
        <p>The final model used four clusters and a fixed seed for reproducibility.</p>
      </div>
      <span class="language-pill">PySpark ML</span>
    </div>

    <div class="code-card">
      <div class="code-toolbar">
        <div class="window-dots"><b></b><b></b><b></b></div>
      </div>
      <pre><code class="language-python"># choose k = 4 (this is reasonable because the roles split into 3 and we can validate it against role_category )
kmeans = KMeans(k=4, featuresCol="scaled_features", seed=42)
model = kmeans.fit(df_model)
df_clustered = model.transform(df_model)</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>


  <div class="ml-subsection">
    <p class="mini-eyebrow">05 — INTERPRETING THE CLUSTERS</p>
    <h3>What did the model actually find?</h3>

    <p class="wide-copy">
      I profiled each cluster by examining its dominant skills and broader job
      characteristics. The resulting groups were interpretable, but they represented
      different levels and types of specialization rather than four perfectly isolated
      occupations.
    </p>

    <div class="cluster-profile-grid">
      <div class="cluster-profile">
        <div class="cluster-number">C0</div>
        <div>
          <span>PROFILE</span>
          <h3>Traditional Analytics</h3>
          <p>SQL-heavy analytical work with less emphasis on advanced ML and statistical skills.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cluster-profile">
        <div class="cluster-number">C1</div>
        <div>
          <span>PROFILE</span>
          <h3>Data Science</h3>
          <p>Greater emphasis on machine learning, statistics, probability, and quantitative depth.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cluster-profile">
        <div class="cluster-number">C2</div>
        <div>
          <span>PROFILE</span>
          <h3>Generalist / Mixed</h3>
          <p>A more heterogeneous group with weaker concentration around one technical specialty.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cluster-profile">
        <div class="cluster-number">C3</div>
        <div>
          <span>PROFILE</span>
          <h3>Business Intelligence</h3>
          <p>Strong SQL combined with BI, dashboarding, and visualization-oriented tools.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>


  <div class="ml-subsection">
    <p class="mini-eyebrow">06 — PCA VISUALIZATION</p>
    <h3>The clusters overlap — and that is part of the result.</h3>

    <p class="wide-copy">
      I used Principal Component Analysis (PCA) to project the high-dimensional model
      space into two dimensions for visualization. The resulting clusters showed
      substantial overlap rather than clean separation.
    </p>

    <div class="ml-code-group">
      <div class="code-card">
        <div class="code-toolbar">
          <div class="window-dots"><b></b><b></b><b></b></div>
          <span class="language-pill">PySpark / PCA</span>
        </div>
        <pre><code class="language-python"># PCA on clustered data
pca_model = pca.fit(df_clustered)
df_pca = pca_model.transform(df_clustered)

# extract x/y
df_pca = df_pca.withColumn("pca_array", vector_to_array(col("pca_features"))) \
               .withColumn("x", col("pca_array")[0]) \
               .withColumn("y", col("pca_array")[1])</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="code-card">
        <div class="code-toolbar">
          <div class="window-dots"><b></b><b></b><b></b></div>
          <span class="language-pill">Matplotlib</span>
        </div>
        <pre><code class="language-python">pdf = df_pca.select("x", "y", "prediction").toPandas()

import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))

for cluster in sorted(pdf["prediction"].unique()):
    subset = pdf[pdf["prediction"] == cluster]
    plt.scatter(subset["x"], subset["y"], label=f"Cluster {cluster}", alpha=0.6)

plt.xlabel("Principal Component 1")
plt.ylabel("Principal Component 2")
plt.title("KMeans Clusters Visualized with PCA")
plt.legend()
plt.show()</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-banner ml-main-finding">
      <span class="finding-kicker">KEY MODEL FINDING</span>
      <strong>Data careers appear to exist on a spectrum.</strong>
      <p>
        The PCA overlap suggests that traditional analytics, BI, data science, and
        AI-oriented work share many underlying capabilities. The roles differ more
        in technical depth and skill composition than through completely rigid
        occupational boundaries.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>


  <div class="ml-subsection">
    <p class="mini-eyebrow">07 — VALIDATING THE INTERPRETATION</p>
    <h3>What else did I compare?</h3>

    <div class="model-validation-grid">
      <div class="validation-card">
        <i class="bi bi-buildings"></i>
        <h3>Industry mix</h3>
        <p>
          No cluster was dominated by a single industry. The mix across sectors
          supports the interpretation that the groups captured transferable skill
          profiles rather than simply separating postings by NAICS sector.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="validation-card">
        <i class="bi bi-currency-dollar"></i>
        <h3>Salary patterns</h3>
